                <pb facs="0823" n="727" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg727"/>

                <head>No. 328.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hunter to Mr. Smyth.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-09-08"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 8,
                        1879</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 35.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your dispatch No. 30, of 30th
                    May last, relative to overtures reported to have been made through the “Liberian
                    consul-general” to the Liberian Government looking to the extension of French
                    protection to that government, I have to inform you that inquiries having been
                    made in high official quarters in regard to the matter, it has been ascertained
                    that the French Government has never proposed or expressed a desire that Liberia
                    should be placed under its protectorate, but has on the contrary declined to
                    entertain any scheme looking to such a result.</p>
                <closer><signed>W. HUNTER,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                        Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
